# What belongs in this repo: everything needed to build the client from
# nothing but a checkout and Docker. That is src/, third_party/ (the vendored
# WebRTC stack and green-nx core), scripts/, tools/, docker/, port/, cmake/
# and docs/.
#
# Everything below is either regenerable, enormous, somebody else's, or a
# place a live credential lands.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local configuration -- READ THIS ONE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Written by scripts/setup-device.sh. Holds the address of somebody's handheld
# and, if they chose password authentication, its password. It is the reason
# nothing in scripts/ carries a default address, host key or password: those
# describe one person's network, and a default that is wrong for everybody but
# its author turns "not configured yet" into a connection failure against a
# stranger's machine.
/device.env
/device.env.bak

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build output
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# 330 MB of binaries and object files, all reproducible from a checkout with
# scripts/build-app.sh and scripts/build-host.sh.
#
# It is ALSO where a real Xbox token lands: the host simulator signs in on its
# own and writes to out/host/state/ (see docs/TESTING.md). That alone is reason
# enough for this line, so do not "just add out/ this once".
/out/

# The built static archives (libpeer, mbedtls, libsrtp2, usrsctp) and their
# installed headers, ~100 MB. Rebuilt by deps/build-deps.sh from the source in
# third_party/, which IS committed. deps/build-deps.sh and deps/patches/ are
# committed; these two output directories are not.
/deps/rg353/
/deps/host/

# The old libpeer clone, superseded by third_party/libpeer. Nothing references
# it and it carries its own .git.
/deps/src/

# CMake configures in-tree, so a build leaves these inside the vendored
# sources. They are output, not content -- see third_party/PROVENANCE.md.
third_party/**/build-rg353/
third_party/**/build-host/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Other people's repositories
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Upstream clones kept for comparison, each with its own .git. Committing them
# would either bury several repos inside this one or create gitlinks pointing
# at commits nobody fetches. NOTHING IS BUILT FROM HERE -- the code we actually
# compile was copied into src/gnx/ and third_party/ -- and the exact upstream
# commit of each is recorded in the two PROVENANCE.md files, so these are
# re-clonable at will.
/reference/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Large binary fixtures
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Stream captures. The synthetic one is regenerated by scripts/mkstream.sh
# (scripts/test.sh does it for you); device captures come back with
# scripts/pull.sh.
#
# A real capture of a live session is also a recording of somebody's gameplay,
# and 49 MB is permanent once committed -- right at GitHub's per-file warning
# threshold. Ignored by default for both reasons. If you would rather have one
# versioned than lose it, that is a defensible call and it is one command:
#
#     git add -f recordings/<name>.xcau
#
recordings/*.xcau

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local notes
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# A scratch directory for working notes, measurements and session logs. The
# reference documentation that ships lives in docs/; this is for the material
# that is genuinely private or genuinely provisional.
/notes/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Local noise
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# A real file called "nul", created when a shell redirect to /dev/null on
# Windows lands on the reserved device name instead. Beyond being junk, a file
# named "nul" CANNOT be checked out on Windows, so committing it would break
# every clone on that platform.
/nul

# Editor and OS droppings.
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp
Thumbs.db
Desktop.ini
.DS_Store

# Leftovers from editing in place.
*.orig
*.rej
*.bak

# Device backups pulled by hand (scripts/pull.sh), e.g. before an install.
gamelist-backup*.xml
